add(2,'2,106,107,108','other',[19,23,31,34,54,58],[(14,42),(173,185),(357,380),(384,407),(849,863),(911,924)],'Using the main result of Murre\'s talk','The aliases, three synthesis tables and bibliographic comparisons are earlier-run apparatus. The manuscript contains Hom, restriction, and finite-étale arguments, but no arithmetic-section, GT-centralizer or Prym-rank result.','Named project statuses remain attributed leads; literature is rechecked in the references/search records of this run.','agrees','The entry distinguishes context from manuscript and cautions against status promotion. Its citations are independently reopened rather than inherited as verified.')
add(3,'3,6,7,8,11,16,17','self-flagged step',[19,20,21,23,31],[(2,113),(147,190),(357,371)],'il suffit pour ceci que P(u_0,S)≠∅; faut préciser un peu','For proper flat finitely presented X,Z over S, separated locally finitely presented Y/S and φ:Z→X, the formally unramified open part of Hom(X,Y)→Hom(Z,Y) is asserted representable by separated unramified morphisms locally of finite presentation. The proposed proof needs existence of graph-ideal lifts, naturality of their Ext classes, and equality with the class measuring compatibility of generic and prescribed lifts.','The modern summary separates three obligations. Neither doubtful verb on scans20–21 is silently resolved, and the P0/P1 ambiguity remains a reading issue. Murre’s locally noetherian criterion is not itself verification of the manuscript’s arbitrary-base hypotheses.','agrees','The letter states the full conclusion; scans19–23 explicitly expose the remaining obstruction comparisons. The earlier notes correctly distinguish separate lifts from compatible lifts and avoid claiming a completed proof.')
add(4,'4','reading question',[19,20],[(8,11),(75,80)],'M_{v_0}→M_{u_0} est injectif','The sentence on scan19 says injective, whereas the exact sequence on scan20 requires the same map to be surjective.','Surjective is mathematically suggested by the tangent-rigidity condition at K91-01 scan16, but no ink reading is changed.','agrees','The contradiction is present in the transcription itself, and the prior note correctly treats it as a scan check.')
add(5,'5','idea of the earlier run',[19],[(19,40)],'0→J→J→J_n→0','Multiplication by the parameter power must be injective on J, and J→Jη must be injective for the stated obstruction reduction. A square-zero thickening need not have an integral ambient ring.','The example R=k[t]_(t), A=R[ε]/ε², J=(ε) is the earlier run’s explicit coefficient test.','agrees','The exact sequence needs regularity on J; integrality of the square-zero thickening would instead force J=0. This is a hypothesis audit, not a new manuscript theorem.')
add(6,'9,12','self-flagged step',[22,24],[(114,125),(193,204)],'Pb Simplifier; si les Hom sont représentatifs','The author proposes removing properness of X,Z and the corepresenting modules by requiring injectivity of tangent restriction for every quasi-coherent coefficient G. The next construction is expressly conditional on representability of the Hom functors.','A properness-free theorem is not supplied; formal unramifiedness alone does not guarantee algebraic or scheme representability.','agrees','Both the question and the later caveat are visible. A07 is reclassified from unproved claim to authorial problem, and A10’s Hom/Ext distinction is retained.',{9:('corrected','The page labels this Pb and adds ?, so record a proposed generalization, not an asserted theorem.'),12:('merged into item 6','The explicit Hom-representability caveat is the unresolved input to the proposed generalization.')})
add(7,'10','withdrawn in the same text',[22,23],[(127,146),(158,190)],'la présence de la condition imposée par v était inessentielle','The deleted generic nonextension argument discards compatibility with the prescribed v; the next live argument formulates compatible splittings instead.','The deletion withdraws this argument, not the later Hom theorem.','agrees','The whole lower draft lies inside deleted; the ensuing compatible-splitting construction explains its logical replacement.')
add(8,'13,14,15','idea of the earlier run',[24,25],[(218,262)],'il f. et s. que; Coker ρ','For q=(i,β):L⊕M′→M surjective and ρ=(σ,σαπ′):L⊕M′→J, pairs (μ,λ) with μq=λρ are represented by C=coker(q,−ρ)≅J/ρ(ker q). The two separate relations R1=σ(ker i), R2=σαπ′(ker β) need not equal ρ(ker q).','The corrected pushout presentation and counterexample are earlier-run mathematics, independently recalculated here; they do not by themselves refute the geometric theorem.','agrees','The manuscript asserts sufficiency of R1+R2 and separately cokerρ. Explicit one-dimensional linear tests refute both abstract identifications; only the corrected fixed-diagram cokernel commutes with arbitrary scalar extension.')
add(9,'18','reading question',[25],[(264,278)],'sép. clos suffit!','Corollary1 states proper X over an algebraically closed field, with a margin allowing separably closed fields, and P/X locally of finite type. Separatedness is not explicitly on this line.','Any separation inherited from the surrounding programme must be stated as an assumption in a modern theorem.','agrees','The margin is a field-hypothesis relaxation, not the alleged exclamation about difficulty. The note preserves the type/presentation revision.')
add(10,'19','unpursued implication',[25,27],[(273,278),(324,326)],'∃y∈x̄ tel que la fibre P_y soit quasi-projective','The positivity claims weaken relative quasi-projectivity to suitable quasi-projective fibres in every associated-point closure; the resulting section scheme is asserted essentially quasi-projective, meaning each quasi-compact open is quasi-projective.','A construction of one essentially ample line bundle and finite probes remains to be supplied; fibrewise positivity is not substituted for a proof.','agrees','The exact associated-point quantifier occurs in the margin, and the note correctly separates it from global quasi-projectivity.')
add(11,'20,21,22,23','unproved claim',[26,31],[(283,320),(369,371)],'X propre et plat de prés. finie; normalement plat le long de Z_i','Let X/S be proper flat finitely presented, P/X locally finitely presented, and finitely many closed Z_i⊂X be flat finitely presented over S. Assume Res_{Z_i/S}(P_Zi) is a scheme, X is normally flat along Z_i, and their union meets the closure of every associated point of every geometric fibre. The text asserts a scheme Res_{X/S}P, locally finitely presented; for quasi-separated S it asserts a quasi-affine restriction map on each quasi-compact open, and a positivity consequence.','The uncertain separatedness conclusion is false without separated P/X: S=X=Z1=Spec k, P the doubled affine line satisfies the displayed assumptions. Fixed finite jets do not detect all infinitesimal maps, so a proposed thickening reconstruction must quantify its order; this does not refute the quasi-affine conclusion.','agrees','The earlier notes accurately distinguish the announced theorem, the uncertain adjective, the quasi-separated insertion and the finite-probe test. No proof is supplied on scans26–32.')
add(12,'24','self-flagged step',[27],[(321,322)],'Les composantes connexes … sont elles de type fini sur k ?','Are connected components of the scheme of sections Res_{X/k}(P/X) of finite type, for proper X/k and locally finite-type P/X in the stated representability setting?','Local finite type alone does not imply finite type of a component; the general wording allows X=Spec k and non-quasi-compact connected P. Projective finite-type targets give bounded Hilbert-polynomial pieces.','agrees','The question is explicit and distinct from representability. The modern test must retain the locally finite-type target hypothesis, not silently strengthen it.')
add(13,'25,27','unproved claim',[27],[(324,334)],'k(x′_i)/k radiciel; extension finie séparable','The field corollaries assert representability given suitable radicial points in each associated-point closure, or after one finite separable extension; over noetherian integral S the assertion shrinks to a nonempty U and allows finite étale U′→U. The cover is omitted only when the generic-fibre point hypothesis holds.','Uniform choice of points and thickening order across all sections is an unproved step; none of these corollaries follows merely from the formal identity Res=Hom.','agrees','Both the radicial condition and separable extension are written. The notes correctly keep shrinking, cover and exceptional no-cover condition distinct.')
