add(15,'28,29,30','unproved claim',[28,32],[(337,342),(376,378)],'along which X is smooth; reduced geometric fibers','The source asserts representability from a smooth section of proper flat X/S with geometrically integral fibres. A smooth-proper connected-fibre corollary gives étale-local existence; the letter broadens it to geometrically reduced fibres. A further paragraph changes to faithfully flat quasi-finite covers and removes smoothness, assuming no embedded associated cycles.','The smooth-point/multisection bridge is a reconstruction. Corollary7’s reversed indices, the reference to result6, and target finiteness letters remain scan questions.','agrees','The prior notes correctly keep the three scopes apart and do not silently infer smoothness from geometric reducedness.')
add(16,'31,33','unproved claim',[28,31,32],[(344,345),(353,380)],'11.9.1967; Dear Anantharaman; Aut is representable','For proper k-schemes X,Y, Isom_k(X,Y), and hence Aut_k(X), is asserted representable; Aut is a group scheme locally of finite type. The letter dated11 September1967 attributes the starting theorem to Matsumura and proposes a systematic relative construction.','Special descent for groups/torsors is needed; ordinary Hom need not descend as a scheme. The printed Matsumura–Oort theorem is independently checked and no priority claim is inferred for undated drafts.','agrees','The letter and corollary agree on Aut/Isom, while the same paragraph denies unrestricted Hom scheme existence.')
add(17,'32','conjecture',[29],[(346,350)],'Il est plausible qu’on puisse … prendre U′=U; cf Raynaud','For proper flat X over a noetherian integral base, the author asks whether Aut becomes a scheme after shrinking alone, without the finite étale cover of Corollary9.','A general group-algebraic-space theorem plus spreading out would address this; Raynaud is a pointer, not an exact theorem citation in the manuscript.','agrees','The proposal is explicitly plausible rather than proved, and the final cover in Corollary9 is finite étale.')
add(18,'34,43,44,45','self-flagged step',[32,35,36],[(374,374),(430,450)],'la descente n’est pas possible; détailler … en lemme','The letter asserts a proper nonprojective target whose Hom from a quadratic field is not a scheme. Scans35–36 propose semilinear descent from a free involution with an orbit admitting no affine neighbourhood, replacing a cancelled base-extension argument by an orbit argument.','Algebraic-space existence is compatible with scheme failure. The named Nagata construction is not identified with a Hironaka example without checking the actual schemes; pointwise action is weaker than schemewise triviality.','agrees','The notes retain the cancelled proof, missing orbit lemma, unfinished K warning and category boundary. The manuscript supplies no full example data here.')
add(19,'35,41','unpursued implication',[32,35],[(378,378),(414,428)],'Hom_S(X,Y)=φ_*(X×Y/X)','For every S-test scheme U, sections of (X×_SY)_U→X_U are naturally maps X_U→Y_U. Likewise Res_{T/S}X is the fibre over id_T of Hom_S(T,X)→Hom_S(T,T).','These are natural bijections of functors, independent of whether any functor is represented; they do not prove geometric representability.','agrees','Both written identities and the letter’s formal reduction agree; the naturality is directly rechecked.')
add(20,'36','unpursued implication',[27,32],[(332,332),(376,378)],'if Y is quasi-projective over S=Spec k','For proper X/k and quasi-projective Y/k, the letter asserts Hom_k(X,Y) is a scheme; X need not be projective.','It is a specialization of the announced section theorem with P=X×Y, not an independent proof on the page.','agrees','The note correctly places positivity on the target and retains properness of the source.')
add(21,'37,73','idea of the earlier run',[23,31,37,38,49],[(158,160),(371,371),(480,480),(499,503),(732,734)],'Greenberg d’égale caractéristique','For B_N=k[t]/t^(N+1), Res_{B_N/k}(Z_BN)(A)=Z(A[t]/t^(N+1)); algebra automorphisms act by precomposition. This provides a precise comparison between finite probes, jets, and later coordinate changes.','The functor comparison is earlier-run mathematics. It does not identify every later module or turn finite-order jets into a Néron theorem.','agrees','The manuscript names differential geometry and Greenberg, and the functor identity is checked directly; the cross-folder comparison still requires both exact definitions.')
add(22,'38,39,40,97,103,104,105','title, date or page count',[25,26,31,32,34,37,50,58],[(264,320),(353,407),(461,496),(753,765),(909,924)],'Schémas Hom; C) Picard; Compléments dans le cas de Greenberg','The interval contains38 retained scan faces: thirteen19–32, sixteen34–49, nine50–58. Scans34 and37 are revised programme sheets, not completed chapters; the latter includes projective-over-proper-flat and Picard headings. Archive foliation is not authorial pagination.','BookI placement is an interpretive lead; no global archive-size estimate follows. The catalogue title is broader than these leaves, which do not construct Néron models or residue pairings.','agrees','All local headings, revisions, date and counts were checked against page markers. The prior note’s separation of local evidence from external planning claims is retained.')
add(23,'42','unproved claim',[35],[(409,433)],'et si on a les conditions d’immersité|immersivité','Given descent datum on X/T for f:T→S, the page proposes X0=Res_fX×_{Res_g(X×_ST)}Res_fX, with g:T×_ST→S and an inserted undeciphered immersion condition.','The two maps and descent topology must be specified. Equality after restriction to the diagonal can reduce the pair to a single compatible section; this is an editorial equalizer explanation, not a proof written here.','agrees','The note rightly preserves the inserted proviso rather than assuming existence of two restrictions suffices under unspecified hypotheses.')
add(24,'46,68','reading question',[38,49],[(499,503),(725,728)],'f_*(V̌(F))=V̌(f_*F)','For finite locally free f:T→S and finite-rank locally free F, with V̌(F)=Spec Sym(F∨) the functor of sections, Res_fV̌(F)≅V̌(f_*F), compatibly with base change.','Finite rank and the section-bundle convention are explicit in the modern statement. The source does not state finite rank, and scan49’s Spec/Sym and dual markings stay unresolved.','agrees','The note correctly prevents a dual-convention mistake or extrapolation to arbitrary infinite-rank bundles.')
add(25,'47,48,49,50','self-flagged step',[39],[(505,531)],'les M_i tendent vers0 dans M (?)','For proper flat f:X→Spec A, A noetherian local, coherent F and coherent K⊂f*F, the text seeks a smallest coherent F1⊂F with K⊂f*F1. It reduces to a complete local base and a filtered intersection, retracting raw sheaf-intersection vanishing in favour of vanishing of coherent subobjects.','For complete A, Chevalley cofinality followed by Krull intersection on the closed fibre and proper support proves the repaired vanishing. The noncomplete opening requires an extra descent argument. The ordinary sheaf intersection of t^iO on Spec k[[t]] is nonzero on D(t), though its quasi-coherent infimum is0.','agrees','The four notes accurately distinguish smallest/greatest, complete/noncomplete, sheaf/quasi-coherent intersections, and the withdrawn stronger assertion.')
add(26,'51,52,53','self-flagged step',[40,41,42],[(546,601)],'Il est évident (préciser); Je dois montrer que ça se recolle','For finite locally free T/S, local representatives Res U_i glue if every relevant geometric section has its entire image inside one U_i. On a test base V the maximal factorization open is V\p(T_V\φ^−1(U_i)), since finite p is closed; this represents the open subfunctor.','The maximal-open formula and overlap proof are modern completion. The finite-extension testing reduction needs finite-presentation hypotheses; the manuscript’s first argument is unfinished.','agrees','Proposition2 assumes existence, Proposition2bis constructs it. The note preserves both jobs and the explicit universal-property gap.')
add(27,'54','withdrawn in the same text',[42,43],[(595,611)],'T/S est géométriquement injectif','Finite locally free universally injective T/S satisfies the affine-image criterion, since each geometric fibre has at most one point. The alternative finite-subset-affine property of X also suffices.','The deletion from the preliminary theorem is relocation into a surviving corollary, not mathematical withdrawal.','agrees','The prior note explicitly makes that distinction; the surviving scan43 statement was read.')
add(28,'55','unproved claim',[43,44],[(611,643)],'f_* est exact à gauche','Restriction, whenever defined as a functor, preserves finite limits. For finite locally free T/S it preserves open/closed immersions and affine morphisms, with representability justified by coefficient equations and the closed-image open criterion.','Adjunction proves finite limits; geometric preservation requires the additional arguments and cannot be inferred solely from left exactness.','agrees','The notes retain the uncertain verification annotation and correctly separate formal identities from geometric claims.')
add(29,'56','idea of the earlier run',[44],[(623,633)],'f_*(X/T)=⋃ f_*(U_i/T)','A finite open cover of X need not induce a cover after restriction. For T=S⊔S, X=T and U_i its two components, Res X=S but each Res U_i=∅.','This earlier-run counterexample applies to an unqualified union rule; the text itself indicates an additional hypothesis, supplied by scans40–43.','agrees','The whole-section image condition is essential; no claim is made that the qualified source assertion is false.')
add(30,'57','unproved claim',[44,45],[(615,648)],'pourvu que f séparable','Finite étale scalar restriction preserves proper and projective morphisms when representatives exist; finite locally free restriction preserves affine and quasi-projective morphisms under the appropriate existence assumptions.','Finite étale is the modern sufficient interpretation of the manuscript’s separable proviso. No proof here removes that proviso.','agrees','The table, locally noetherian margin, Chow route and missing-detail request were all read; the note accurately retains them.')
add(31,'58','idea of the earlier run',[44],[(635,641)],'si X′→X est surjectif, alors f_*(X′)→f_*(X) l’est','For finite locally free f, bare surjectivity need not survive restriction: Spec k→Spec k[ε]/ε² is surjective but has no section after any nonempty k-base change. Finite étale f or smooth surjective g restores the relevant assertion.','The counterexample targets only the unrestricted rule; the page’s separable Chow context is not refuted.','agrees','The nilpotent must simultaneously vanish and remain nonzero in a section, which directly verifies the obstruction.')
add(32,'59','conjecture',[44],[(641,641)],'fidèlement plat non fini','The author asks whether properness descends along faithfully flat non-finite maps and answers affirmatively under a locally noetherian assumption.','Modern fpqc descent proves the assertion without noetherianness for fpqc covers; the written non-finite condition alone does not provide quasi-compactness.','agrees','The note corrects Hua’s alleged fini to the actual non fini, and retains the necessary topology boundary.')
add(33,'60','idea of the earlier run',[44,45],[(638,638),(648,654)],'det f_*(L)','For p:R×_ST→R and evaluation ev, the normalized norm of ev*L is det p*ev*L⊗(det p*O)^−1. The second factor comes from S and does not alter relative ampleness.','The normalization is earlier-run clarification, not the notation as written.','agrees','The literal determinant lacks the normalizing factor. Transition functions and finite locally free determinant construction verify the distinction.')
add(34,'61,62','unproved claim',[45,46,47,48],[(650,670),(687,702)],'ample (resp. très ample); immersion projective fermée','The source asserts determinant-line ampleness/very ampleness under scalar restriction, then claims a closed projective embedding. The ensuing construction only gives an open locus inside a closed Grassmannian condition.','For dual numbers, Res(P1)=T(P1), with a closed A1 fibre, is not proper and cannot embed closed in projective space. This refutes arbitrary finite-flat closedness, not necessarily the ample-line assertion or the finite étale case.','agrees','The notes correctly separate very ampleness via immersion from the stronger closedness step and track deletion of fermé.')
add(35,'63,64,65','self-flagged step',[46,47,48],[(667,702)],'φ_{iλμ}=0; det(u_ξ) soit inversible','For finite free A-algebra B of rank d and an A-locally split quotient M of B^n of A-rank kd, the B-stability condition is closed, and the locus where M is B-locally free of rank k is open, detected by determinants of B^k→M.','Coefficient vanishing is scheme-theoretic after arbitrary base change; denominators can be cleared locally. This gives locally closed, not generally closed, Grassmannian locus.','agrees','The prior notes preserve quotient-order/rank doubts and distinguish the universal stability equations from a field-point test.')
add(36,'66,67','self-flagged step',[48,49],[(704,728)],'rédiger convenablement','For finite locally free T/S of bounded rank and finite-type X/T over locally noetherian S, the affine-image criterion yields a finite family of affine opens capturing every section image; hence its scheme restriction is finite type.','After reduction to noetherian affine S and rank d>0, use the quasi-compact open ⋃U^d⊂X^d and repeat support points to d-tuples. Rank0 is immediate.','agrees','The note correctly makes noetherianness, bounded support and one-fibre tuples explicit rather than relying on an ordinary finite affine cover.')
add(37,'69,70','withdrawn in the same text',[49],[(732,734)],'Il n’est pas vrai en général que la dim … soit majorée','Without smoothness, scalar restriction can have positive dimension when the target’s relative fibres have dimension0. For the editorial dual-number example End(k[ε]/ε²)=Spec k[a,b]/(a²,2ab); in characteristic≠2 its automorphism locus is G_m.','The dual-number specification is deleted on the page; its complete equations are a prior-run test, not the surviving source’s chosen example.','agrees','The note correctly includes the mixed equation2ab and preserves the cancellation.')
add(38,'71','idea of the earlier run',[47],[(697,702)],'det(u_ξ) soit inversible','The determinant-open freeness test parallels maximal-minor surjectivity tests for rigidification and the later requirement that a coordinate-change determinant be a unit over a general ring.','This is a method comparison; nonzero determinant over Z does not imply invertibility, but no unidentified later module is resolved.','agrees','The page requires a unit, and the note does not conflate rectangular surjectivity with square invertibility.')
add(39,'72,96','idea of the earlier run',[35,44,55,56],[(414,428),(623,625),(873,884)],'f_*(X×_T Y/T)=f_*(X/T)×_S f_*(Y/T)','Products and group structures under finite separable restriction give a framework for the earlier project’s claimed Weil-restriction isogenies.','SECTIONS_OVER_Q A.185 is only an attributed prior-run lead; the three curves, fields, maps and rank-eight computation are absent and not verified here.','agrees','The prior notes explicitly deny re-proving the arithmetic result; manuscript content supports only the formal comparison.')
add(40,'74,75','self-flagged step',[50,51],[(753,779)],'Théorème?; Supposons T/S affine','Assume the restriction morphism exists and is locally of finite presentation. If T/S is affine and g is smooth, formal lifting on affine pullbacks proves its smoothness; if g is étale, unique lifts glue without affineness and prove formal étaleness, hence étaleness.','These are modern formal-lifting verifications. The manuscript’s locally noetherian/local finite-type hypotheses supply finite presentation; its numerical section argument is not needed.','agrees','The affine insertion is specific to smoothness. The note correctly distinguishes the source’s proof from a cleaner modern proof.')
add(41,'76,77','idea of the earlier run',[51,52],[(773,784)],'le relèvement est connu dès qu’on connaît l’image d’un pt','The one-point section-determination argument needs separatedness. Glue two copies of A1 along A1\{0,1}; the étale map has maximal fibre size2 over connected T, while its section scheme is four points, contradicting the unqualified2^1 bound.','The explicit nonseparated example is earlier-run mathematics independently checked; it does not refute preservation of étaleness.','agrees','All visible finiteness/representability conditions hold for the example, and the note correctly isolates the missing separation hypothesis.')
add(42,'78,79,98','reading question',[52,53],[(800,825)],'Alors D(s)=nD′(s)','The written dimension-set equation is reversed even for restriction of A1 along dual numbers. Simply reversing it also fails for mixed dimensions: two source points with dimensions{1,2} produce{2,3,4}; even a connected source can have target components never reached by sections.','For smooth g of pure relative dimension d and finite locally free T/S of rank n, restriction has pure relative dimension nd wherever represented. More generally the dimension at a section is a length-weighted sum of dimensions along that actual section, not the unqualified set equality.','agrees','The prior notes correctly distinguish the false literal formula, insufficient repairs, and valid pure-dimensional consequence.')
