add(44,'81','idea of the earlier run',[54],[(840,847)],'exactement λ^{ν_s} points','If T/S is finite locally free, restriction of finite étale g of rankλ has fibre rankλ^{ν_s}. With the visible deletion of finiteness the formula fails: squaring G_m→G_m over algebraically closed k, char≠2, has no global section, although T=G_m is affine finite type and connected.','The empty restriction scheme is verified by Laurent-exponent parity after every geometric base change.','agrees','The example satisfies the broadened standing assumptions and pinpoints finiteness of the source as the lost input.')
add(45,'82,83,84','unproved claim',[54,55],[(849,866)],'Conditions équivalentes; est fini sur S ?','For finite locally free f:T→S the following are equivalent: ν_s=#π0(T_sbar) is locally constant; Res preserves finite étale morphisms; it sends every finite étale T-object to a finite étale S-object; Res(T⊔T) is finite over S. Such f is called homogeneous, and this class is stable under base change and composition.','The complete proof in this run is a modern verification, not a transcribed proof: étale-locally split f into finite flat universal homeomorphisms; topological invariance gives preservation. The binary test has2^{ν_s} geometric points, so finite étale rank detects ν. The broader deleted-finite scope is not certified.','agrees','The note gives all four quantifiers correctly and retains the question mark and finite-case qualification.')
add(46,'85,87','idea of the earlier run',[52,54,55,56],[(800,804),(830,866),(879,884)],'n=deg T/S; ν_s','Length and geometric component count govern different effects: dual numbers double smooth affine dimension but do not square the cardinality of constant finite étale objects. For μ2/Z2, ν is2 generically and1 specially, so the binary restriction has4 and2 geometric points and is not finite.','The example is a caution for LM1 D.11, not a result about that project’s integral extensions.','agrees','The prior notes’ numerical distinction and finite-rank obstruction were independently checked.')
add(47,'86,88','unpursued implication',[55,56],[(871,884)],'fibrés faibles principaux; de groupe f_*(Y/T)×_S f_*(T×G/T)','Restriction preserves the principal-action identity by finite limits. For finite source and smooth surjective torsors, smooth surjectivity supplies local sections; the restricted constant finite group over a geometric point is G^{ν_s}. Homogeneity makes its rank finite étale locally constant for nontrivial G.','The topology and represented objects must be specified; weak principal action is not by itself a torsor.','agrees','The notes correctly distinguish the changed group, base change, and local-existence obligation.')
add(48,'89','withdrawn in the same text',[56,57],[(886,907)],'petit raisonnement; Plus généralement','The cancelled attempt corepresents a functor on ordinary finite sets, then adds π1-action; the live route begins with finite π1-sets.','These are different assertions; a counterexample to all left-exact equivariant endofunctors does not by itself refute the special geometric functor.','agrees','The cancellation and transition were read and preserved.')
add(49,'90,92,93,94,99','self-flagged step',[57,58],[(903,924)],'Ce qui ne devrait pas être nécessaire; un pt de compatibilité à établir','The intended finite étale component object A(T) should give internal Hom_S(A(T),E)≅internal Hom_S(T,E), compatibly with étale localization, and hence a canonical T→A(T). For finite locally free homogeneous T/S it is the finite étale component quotient; ordinary global-Hom representability is weaker.','Over S=Spec C[[t]], T=Spec C[[t]][x]/(x²−t), A=S represents maps into constant finite étale targets, but after geometric generic base change T has two components. Thus the global universal property does not certify the internal one. The sentence extracting the canonical map continues at scan59, assigned to the next package.','agrees','The notes preserve the choice-of-basepoint reservation and internal/global distinction. Their claim that scan59 was unavailable is updated to an inter-package continuation, not an evidence gap in the present archive.',{94:('corrected','The original package ended at58, but the present archive contains scan59 in MPL_91_059-098; its continuation is checked by the neighbouring segment.')})
add(50,'91','idea of the earlier run',[58],[(911,917)],'un tel foncteur est nécessairement de la forme Hom̲_{π1}(A,E)','Left exactness of an endofunctor of finite G-sets does not force it to be an internal-Hom functor. For G=C2, replace every action by the trivial action on its underlying set; this preserves finite limits, finite colimits and surjections but cannot be E↦E^A.','Evaluation on the trivial two-point object forces|A|=1; then internal Hom is the identity, contradicted by the regular two-point G-set. Additional étale-local/base-change compatibility is needed.','agrees','The counterexample was checked on the two decisive objects and correctly targets the broad categorical premise only.')
add(51,'95','reading question',[55,56],[(875,882)],'Dans le résumé … T/S fini','The difficult margin may restrict T/S to finite in a summary, rather than discuss principal-homogeneous terminology; the clipped phrase at55–56 is uncertain.','No polished list of torsor preservation properties is substituted for the uncertain transition.','agrees','The earlier note reports competing readings with low visual confidence, not a resolved contradiction.')
add(52,'100,101,102','other',[19,22,23,27,29,35,36,37,39,40,41,42,44,45,47,48,50,51,54,55,56,57,58],[(2,924)],'à détailler; préciser; est fini sur S ?','These three index entries repeat the preceding authorial questions, withdrawals and organizational marks; they introduce no additional theorem.','They are consolidated as navigation metadata; the live problems and scan questions are represented separately, rather than counted again.','agrees','Every listed quotation was located in the transcription. OK, on sait and c’est formel are not automatically reclassified as doubts; genuinely deleted and uncertain marks remain distinguished.')

# Codex-only material: no verdict rows, because the source index excludes these leads.
add(53,'','title, date or page count',[2,3],[(1,27)],'Représentabilité des Foncteurs Hom; sans hyp. projectives','The two title sheets announce representability, relative products, Greenberg, flattening, quotients, Hom and Aut; no manuscript date or page total is written.','These are the programme’s own words, independent of later project lenses.','agrees','Read both title sheets in K91-01; the apparent archive sequence is not an authored pagination.',file=K)
add(54,'','unproved claim',[5,7,8],[(28,108)],'Pour que … soit représentable … commute aux limites projectives adiques','For X/S flat finite type with S locally noetherian and Y→X a closed immersion, the section subfunctor of S is a closed subscheme iff it commutes with adic inverse limits of Artinian rings. Proper X satisfies the condition. The page also proposes the broader monomorphism/immersion variants.','Closed immersion and general monomorphism versions must not be identified; the printed Murre Theorem3 verifies the former. The affine flatness and schematic-density steps are proof sketches.','agrees','The 8-condition representability audit is read through scan8; deleted alternatives and the broader margin are retained as such.',file=K)
add(55,'','unproved claim',[8,9,10],[(100,135)],'Condition R; Cent_G(u) est représentable','Condition R says an open subgroup of G_T containing the special fibre, for every local T/S, equals G_T. For finite-type groups over locally noetherian S the text reduces testing to DVRs, gives connected-fibre/finite-subschema-cover sufficient cases, and derives transporter, centralizer and normalizer representability under flatness and R, with closedness qualifications.','A modern safe formulation uses SGA3’s purity/essential-freeness hypotheses; the exact scope of every cancelled variant is not broadened.','agrees','The closedness of centralizers depends explicitly on separated G, and flat H is inserted in the live theorem.',file=K)
add(56,'','unproved claim',[11,12],[(137,185)],'Le foncteur … soit représentable … limites projectives adiques','For finite-type X/S with S locally noetherian and coherent E, the flattening (or faithful-flattening) subfunctor is asserted representable iff compatible with adic limits of Artinian rings; proper X and the stated group/R case are given as sufficient cases.','The formula’s F_T for E_T and generic/special-fibre wording are transcription questions. The modern statement distinguishes coherent sheaf flatness from flatness of X itself.','agrees','The statement and all 8 criterion checks are present as a sketch; Murre Theorem2 supplies the exact published comparison.',file=K)
add(57,'','self-flagged step',[12,13],[(187,212)],'X×_S X→X est plat; Reste à prouver','For finitely presented f:X→S, flatness of pr1:X×_S X→X is asserted equivalent to factorization X→S′→S with the first map faithfully flat and the second a monomorphism; S′ is unique and finitely presented.','The manuscript reduces to the flattening criterion after a noetherian approximation. The locally noetherian finite-type printed counterpart is Murre §3A, Corollary2.','agrees','The outstanding implication is explicitly marked and then addressed; it is not left unmentioned after the first Reste line.',file=K)
add(58,'','unproved claim',[14],[(214,218)],'P soit form. pr. hom. sous G','A finitely presented formal principal homogeneous G-space P is claimed to become an fppf torsor over a unique finitely presented monomorphism S′→S.','Flatness of G/S is needed for the implication through the preceding criterion. Without an inherited flatness assumption, G=Spec k[t,x]/(tx), P=G under translation has the principal-action isomorphism and a section but is not flat, so S′ would have to equal S and the conclusion fails.','overstates','The visible scan14 does not restate flatness of G. This is a mathematical hypothesis alert, not a correction of its reading.',file=K)
add(59,'','unproved claim',[14],[(220,222)],'Ker u=N … plat … G/N existe','For a finitely presented homomorphism u:G→H of group schemes with flat finitely presented kernel N, the fppf quotient G/N is asserted to exist as a scheme.','The representability of the image is the previous flat-equivalence-relation argument; the base-category and finite-presentation hypotheses remain essential.','agrees','The kernel flatness is written and the result matches the published application following Murre’s Corollary2 in the locally noetherian setting.',file=K)
add(60,'','unproved claim',[16,17,18],[(223,315)],'M_w→M_u … surjectif; par Nakayama','For proper flat X,Z over locally noetherian S and finite-type Y/S, tangent Hom functors in quasi-coherent coefficients are corepresented by coherent modules compatible with base change. Surjectivity M_v→M_u defines the open formally unramified locus.','Separatedness is queried on scan16 and later fixed in the letter. The formal torsor square on18 proves a conditional compatibility statement only after existence of a lift, as scan19 explicitly recognizes.','agrees','The modules, coefficient quantifier, Nakayama openness, theorem and beginning of the obstruction proof were read together with their continuation.',file=K)

